course: dictionary
lesson: 13791
title: Invading and taking over
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Invading means going into a place where you are not wanted, often by force or in large numbers. Context words give you clues. «The invading army crossed the river at dawn.» Army and crossed the river tell you the soldiers are coming in to take control.
explain: Invading can also describe germs or weeds. «Germs were invading Sam's body, so he felt ill.» Body and ill tell you the germs are getting in and causing harm. Always ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Invading can mean going into someone's private space without permission. «Stop invading my privacy and read your own book!» Privacy is the clue: you are not allowed to look at someone's things without asking.
fact: Invading means entering a place by force, or coming in where you are not wanted.
fact: Invading is the -ing form of the verb invade: invade, invades, invaded, invading.
fact: Invading armies have taken control of countries, such as the Romans, Vikings and Normans invading Britain.
fact: In science, germs can invade the body and make you ill, and some plants invade habitats where they are not meant to grow.
fact: A good check is to swap in "coming in where you are not wanted" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The invading army marched towards the city.» What does invading mean here? | Sleeping under the stars | Leaving the city in a hurry | *Entering a country by force to take control | Singing and dancing in the streets
exercise: Mum says germs are invading Ben's body, so he feels poorly. What does invading mean? | Growing into a big tree | Going to bed early | *Getting into the body and causing harm | Eating a healthy breakfast
exercise: «Ants were invading the kitchen after the picnic.» What does invading mean here? | Cleaning up crumbs carefully | *Coming in large numbers where they are not wanted | Hiding under the table | Going outside for a walk
test: Sofia says, «Stop invading my privacy, read your own diary!» What does invading my privacy mean? | Buying her a birthday present | Keeping her company while she reads | *Going into her private things without being asked | Helping her with her homework
test: Which word fits best? «Long ago, the Vikings were ___ Britain.» | smiling | swimming | sleeping | *invading
exam: «Weeds were invading the vegetable garden, so Grandad pulled them out.» What does invading mean? | Watering the plants carefully | Picking vegetables for dinner | Planting new seeds in neat rows | *Growing in large numbers where they are not wanted
